

Setting up Kali Linux in VirtualBox was a new and slightly challenging experience for me.
I started by downloading Oracle VirtualBox and the Kali Linux VirtualBox image from the
official website. At first, I was confused because the downloaded Kali file didn't have
a `.7z` or `.zip` extension, but it showed two files inside: a `.vbox` and a `.vdi` file.
I initially thought I had the wrong file, but later realized it was already a compressed
archive that looked like a folder. Since 7-Zip was not properly installed on my system,
I couldn't see the “Extract” option when I right-clicked. To fix this, I reinstalled 7-Zip 
and then successfully extracted the Kali image. After extraction, I double-clicked the `.
vbox` file, which automatically opened VirtualBox and added Kali Linux to it. While setting
up the VM, I was unsure how many CPUs to allocate and chose 2 for better performance. I also
faced trouble locating the `.vdi` file during setup, until I realized it was stored inside a
nested folder. Once I started the VM, I logged in using the default credentials (`kali/kali`).
I was confused about how to shut it down, and learned that selecting “Save the machine state”
is safe and doesn’t drain battery. Finally, as per my teacher’s instructions, I created a
dedicated folder in my main drive to store all my Kali-related codes. Overall, this process
taught me how to carefully troubleshoot issues, understand how VirtualBox handles virtual
machines, and organize my workspace efficiently.
